Date: Wed, 3 Mar 93 14:14:43 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL: extracting compensation

Peter Cappello forwards Adam Buchsbaum's question:

>If prostitution, surrogate child bearing, selling body parts for
>cash, etc. is all legal (and, as we all know, they should be), in
>short, if everything has a market value, cannot a court order
>such things sold to repay a debt or to recompense a victim?

To which Thomas Burns responds: 

>One can imagine PPLs that allow such seizure, as well as slavery and
>murder contracts, but would anyone want to live that way? I think that
>would make the enforcers too powerful, corruptable, and state-like. A
>free market seems unlikely to select such a scheme.

I would put it this way.  A compensation scheme should try and give
the least punishment for a fixed restitution, or try and give the most
restitution for a fixed punishment, however you want to look at it.
The natural way to do this is to require a given fine from the
criminal paid to the victim.  

What if the criminal cannot pay the fine without selling things they
value very dearly, like their favorite car, their sexual favors, or
their liver?  Then clearly the punishment imposed by this fine becomes
very high.  I see two natural responses.  Either the fine is too high,
imposing punishment in excess of that needed to deter criminals from
such crimes, or it is not too high, being what the original customers
of the law expected was appropriate to deter crime.

If the fine is too high, then the appropriate answer is to lower it.
If it is not too high, leave it be.  In either case I don't see why we
should limit the kinds of ways someone could gather funds to pay the
fine.  As long as someone is going to be required to pay that size of
fine, I don't think it does them any favors to tell them they can't
sell their sexual favors or right arm, and therefore must be placed in
a labor prison for 20 years.  As long as they pay the court-ordered
fine to the victim, why not let them figure out what would cause them
the least harm?

Robin Hanson

P.S. Many thanks to Harry S. for finding that great article on
Privatization.

