From: "W. Scott Meeks" <meeks@osf.org>
Subject: POLI: "Fairness"

>Date: Wed, 3 Mar 93 12:56:47 EST
>From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
>   >Last week I had an interesting encounter in the local market.
>   >After waiting too long in the middle of a long line, the aisle next to me
>   >opened up, as I was moving my shopping cart over to take advantage of the 
>   >opening a young guy on the end of the line was moving in quickly to 
>   >cut others off. I suggested to him that it might be more fair to go
>   >in the order we had already established, [...]
>
>As extropians, is there any reason for us to discuss "fairness"?
>Isn't the elimination of "fairness" as an organizational principle the
>*essence* of libertarian social organization?  Isn't "fairness" the
>ultimate weapon of liberal-weenies to oppose the free market system?
>
>(I'm actually serious about this!)
>
>Dale

I think this is a terminology problem; "fair" has been twisted by the
promulgators of NewSpeak to become synonymous with "egalitarian".  It's
this version of "fair" (switching to General Semantics) or fair(2) that the
liberal-weenies use to bash free-marketeers.  Fair(1) I take to mean
something more like "consistent with rules and/or free from improper
influence".  This would seem to make fair(1) more synonymous with
utilitarian(1).  (I assume here some function of a linear combination of
individual utility functions.  A separate argument can be made as to whether the
global utility function should be some more complex function of the
individual functions.)

I would argue that fairness(1) is actually one of the goals of libertarian
social organization (at least of utilitarian libertarianism) and that free
markets are in many cases the most fair(1) way of performing societal
functions such as distribution of resources.  

So for the supermarket example above, my intuitive estimate of the utility
functions of the parties involved suggest that maintaining the established
order maximized the utility of the individuals involved and thus might be
considered more fair(1).

However, all this being said, I still have some trepidation about the
supermarket example.  In particular, the narrator (I've lost the
attributions and don't remember who it was) could be considered to have
initiated force against the "young man" first by implied threat of physical
violence and second by pushing the cart into the "young man's" midsection.
Intuitively and from a utilitarian viewpoint, the narrator seems to have
"done the right thing",  but does seem like he might have violated
the non-coercion rule-of-thumb.

	Scott

